WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.812 --> 00:00:11.967
OLIVIA: Hello, Little Fox readers, and welcome to the latest edition of People in the

2
00:00:11.968 --> 00:00:17.123
News . Today Ben will be introducing us to Malala Yousafzai. She’s the teenager who was

3
00:00:17.124 --> 00:00:23.945
shot in 2012 for defending the rights of girls to attend school. Two years later,

4
00:00:23.946 --> 00:00:30.944
at age 17, she became the youngest person to win the Nobel Peace Prize. Malala

5
00:00:30.945 --> 00:00:35.784
certainly doesn’t sound like a typical high school student, Ben! BEN: She's not, Olivia. But in

6
00:00:35.785 --> 00:00:41.529
some ways she’s just like other kids! Malala was born on July 12, 1997, in

7
00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:51.577
the Swat Valley of Pakistan. Her father was an educator who founded three schools. From

8
00:00:51.578 --> 00:00:55.829
an early age, Malala was eager to attend school. Once she started, she became an excellent—and

9
00:00:55.830 --> 00:01:04.233
very competitive—student. She always wanted to be first in her class. Malala was also an

10
00:01:04.234 --> 00:01:15.253
observant child. She noticed that men and women were treated differently in Pakistan, and that

11
00:01:15.254 --> 00:01:22.658
didn’t seem fair. Women were expected to stay home and take care of their families.

12
00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:30.229
They had to wear veils to hide their faces from men. When Malala announced that she

13
00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:36.394
would never cover her face, her mother was horrified, but her father supported her. While

14
00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:44.131
Malala was growing up, the Taliban was gaining control of the area where Malala lived.

15
00:01:44.132 --> 00:01:50.632
The Taliban is an Islamic militant group that interprets Islamic law very strictly, and restricts women's

16
00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:58.537
activities. In 2007 a Taliban leader declared that local women were not allowed to go

17
00:01:58.538 --> 00:02:04.433
out in public. The Taliban also wanted the girls to stop going to school. Everyone

18
00:02:04.434 --> 00:02:07.460
was forbidden to watch TV and movies. At night Malala could hear the sounds of bombs

19
00:02:07.461 --> 00:02:13.194
exploding and machine guns firing. OLIVIA: That sounds really frightening! BEN: It was. The Pakistani

20
00:02:13.195 --> 00:02:18.344
army tried to force the Taliban out of the Swat Valley. Finally, after a year

21
00:02:18.345 --> 00:02:23.023
and a half, the Taliban went into hiding. But the Taliban soon struck back with a

22
00:02:23.024 --> 00:02:29.173
series of terrorist attacks. In 2008 they bombed two hundred schools and killed many individuals

23
00:02:29.174 --> 00:02:34.521
who stood up to them. Malala worried about her father because he’d been receiving threatening

24
00:02:34.522 --> 00:02:40.583
letters. The Taliban considered his schools, which educated females, an insult to Islam. Malala’s father also

25
00:02:40.584 --> 00:02:45.007
spoke out against the school bombings. With his encouragement, Malala began to make speeches too,

26
00:02:45.008 --> 00:02:53.238
which were broadcast on TV and radio, and reported in newspapers. In December 2008 the

27
00:02:53.239 --> 00:03:01.731
Taliban ordered all girls in Swat to stop going to school as of January 15,

28
00:03:01.732 --> 00:03:06.324
2009. This meant that fifty thousand girls couldn’t attend school anymore! Even before the deadline, many

29
00:03:06.325 --> 00:03:11.893
girls stopped going to school because they were afraid. Meanwhile the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC)

30
00:03:11.894 --> 00:03:22.459
asked Malala’s father if he knew of a teacher or high school student willing to

31
00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:30.134
blog about life under the Taliban. But everyone was too afraid of the Taliban to write

32
00:03:30.135 --> 00:03:34.559
about them—until 11-year-old Malala volunteered for the job. The BBC insisted she use a pseudonym

33
00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:41.384
to hide her identity. In two months she wrote 35 blog entries; she also continued

34
00:03:41.385 --> 00:03:44.801
to speak against the Taliban. The BBC made two documentaries about her. By the end of

35
00:03:44.802 --> 00:03:50.362
2009, everyone realized that Malala was the BBC blogger. Over the next two years, she

36
00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:54.358
got even more attention. She received Pakistan’s first National Youth Peace Prize, which was later

37
00:03:54.359 --> 00:04:01.594
renamed the Malala Prize. She was also nominated for the International Children’s Peace Prize. But the

38
00:04:01.595 --> 00:04:06.362
Taliban also noticed her, and she began receiving death threats from them. OLIVIA: So did

39
00:04:06.363 --> 00:04:11.434
she stop speaking out about girls’ rights? BEN: Not at all. She believed that this

40
00:04:11.435 --> 00:04:15.411
was her mission in life, so she stayed calm. Her father, however, was very upset. He

41
00:04:15.412 --> 00:04:23.834
couldn’t understand how the Taliban could threaten a child. In October 2012 Malala was on

42
00:04:23.835 --> 00:04:27.569
her way home from school when she was shot by a Taliban gunman. Malala’s injuries

43
00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:34.837
were so serious that she was evacuated to England, so she could receive better medical

44
00:04:34.838 --> 00:04:41.979
care. Her family decided to stay in England because it was too dangerous to return to

45
00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:48.319
Pakistan. Today Malala has no memory of the attack. But she does remember her long

46
00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:57.798
hospital stay and the thousands of encouraging letters she received. And after her injury, the

47
00:04:57.799 --> 00:05:04.630
issue of girls’ education got a lot more international attention. The Malala Fund was set up

48
00:05:04.631 --> 00:05:09.811
to educate girls in Pakistan and around the world. Nine months after the shooting, Malala

49
00:05:09.812 --> 00:05:17.389
made her first public appearance when she addressed the United Nations in New York City.

50
00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:24.381
It was her sixteenth birthday. Later that year she received the United Nation's Human Rights Prize.

51
00:05:24.382 --> 00:05:28.711
She was also nominated for the Nobel Peace Prize but didn’t win. Meanwhile Malala wrote

52
00:05:28.712 --> 00:05:35.934
an autobiography, I Am Malala , and continued to be in demand as a speaker.

53
00:05:35.935 --> 00:05:43.449
As she notes in her book, she enjoyed speaking, but it created a problem: she sometimes

54
00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:48.861
fell behind in her schoolwork! In 2014 Malala was nominated again for the Nobel Peace

55
00:05:48.862 --> 00:05:56.528
Prize. She was in chemistry class when she found out that she’d won, along with

56
00:05:56.529 --> 00:06:02.668
Kailash Satyarthi, a children’s rights activist. Malala donated her share of the $1.1 million prize to

57
00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:10.423
the Malala Fund. Some people have said that Malala’s father used her to promote his

58
00:06:10.424 --> 00:06:15.739
own ideas when she was too young to understand the consequences. But Malala doesn’t blame

59
00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:23.079
him for the shooting; she wanted to speak out for girls. At the Nobel Prize

60
00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:27.234
award ceremony, she said, "I will continue this fight until I see every child in school."

61
00:06:27.235 --> 00:06:34.136
She’d like to be Pakistan’s prime minister someday! Malala is an amazing teenager, Olivia, but

62
00:06:34.137 --> 00:06:39.469
in some ways she's just like a regular kid. She loves cupcakes and pizza, and

63
00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:47.152
finds her two younger brothers annoying! OLIVIA: Wow, Ben. I always complain about going to school,

64
00:06:47.153 --> 00:06:52.603
but Malala's story really makes me appreciate it! See you next week, Little Fox readers.

